1. Introduction to Functional Testing

​Functional Testing is a key subset of System Testing, alongside GUI Testing, Usability Testing, and Non-Functional Testing.
• ​Definition: Functionality refers to the behavior of the application.
• ​Core Concept: Functional testing focuses specifically on how your features should work based on user requirements.
​
2. Core Functional Testing Components

​functional testing comprises six core areas.
The details for each are expanded below:

​A. Object Properties Testing
• ​Purpose: Check the properties of objects present on the application interface.
• ​Examples of what to verify: Whether UI elements are enabled, disabled, visible, or have focus appropriately.


​B. Database Testing / Backend Testing
• ​Data Manipulation Language (DML) Operations: Verifying backend actions including INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, and SELECT.
• ​Table & Column Level Validations: Checking column types, column length, and the total number of columns .
• ​Schema & Relational Integrity: Validating the relationship between tables, including database Normalization.
• ​Database Objects: Testing database-specific programming elements such as Functions, Procedures, Triggers, Indexes, and Views.

​C. Error Handling Testing
• ​Objective: Testers verify that the application properly displays error messages when incorrect actions or inputs occur .
•  ​Requirements for Error Messages:
◦ ​Must be readable and written in user-understandable/simple language.
◦ ​Should handle specific conditions like Incorrect data and an Invalid user login attempt.

​D. Calculations / Manipulations Testing
• ​Objective: The tester should explicitly verify all calculation logic and mathematical rules executed within the application.

​E. Links (Existence & Execution) Testing
• ​Links Existence: Verifying where exactly the links are placed visually on the web page.
• ​Links Execution: Checking if clicking the links navigates the user to the correct, intended page .
• ​Types of Links to Test: Internal links, external links, and searching for any broken links .

​F. Cookies & Sessions Testing
• ​Cookies: Verifying the temporary files created by the web browser while a user browses pages on the internet .
• ​Sessions: Validating time slots created by the server. Testing must verify that a session properly expires after a period of idle time (if a user is idle for some time).

​3. Non-Functional Testing Components
​Non-functional testing shifts the focus away from behavior to evaluate operational characteristics.

​A. Performance Testing
​Focuses on evaluating the overall speed and stability of the application.
• ​Load Testing: Gradually increasing the user load on the application to observe and check its speed.
• ​Stress Testing: Suddenly increasing or decreasing the load on the application to check how it handles extreme fluctuations.
• ​Volume Testing: Checking how much data the system is able to handle efficiently.

​B. Security Testing
​Evaluates how secure the application is against potential threats.
• ​Authentication: Verifying whether users are valid or not.
• ​Authorization / Access Control: Verifying specific permissions assigned to a valid user.

​C. Recovery Testing
• ​Objective: Verifying the system’s ability to change back from an abnormal state to a normal state after a crash or failure.

​D. Compatibility Testing
​Checking how the application functions across different operating environments.
• ​Forward Compatibility.
• ​Backward Compatibility.
• ​Hardware Compatibility / Configuration Testing.

​E. Installation Testing
• ​Checks if installation screens are clear and easy to understand .
• ​Validates screen navigation flow during setup.
• ​Ensures process clarity (simple or not) and checks un-installation routines.

​F. Sanitation / Garbage Testing
• ​Rule: If any application provides extra, unrequested features or functionality beyond the initial project requirements, it is considered a bug

